---
import { CONTACT } from "../../i18n/locale";
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import { CITIES, CRITERIA, CRITERION_KEYS } from "../../lib/cities";
import { MUSIC } from "../../lib/music";
import { criterionCopy } from "../../i18n/city-copy";
import { countryName } from "../../i18n/places";

// Mirrors /methode: same figures, computed from the same data, so both pages always agree.
const scores = CITIES.flatMap((city) => CRITERION_KEYS.map((key) => city.scores[key]));
const unknown = scores.filter((score) => score.level === null).length;
const estimates = scores.filter((s) => s.level !== null && s.confidence === "editorial").length;
const percentOf = (n: number) => Math.round((100 * n) / scores.length);
const advisedAgainst = CITIES.filter((city) => city.advisedAgainst).length;

const criteria = CRITERIA.map((criterion) => criterionCopy(criterion, "en"));
// The grids fall back to French until src/data/en/criteria.json holds their translation.
const gridsInFrench = criteria.some((criterion, i) => criterion.means === CRITERIA[i].means);

// Genre names stay as the dance floors say them; only the French descriptive one changes.
const GENRES_EN: Record<string, string> = {
  "Rumba congolaise et ndombolo": "Congolese rumba and ndombolo",
};
---

<Page
  title="Our method"
  description="How Over There scores the cities, where the figures come from and what they do not say."
>
  <h1>Our method</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Over There compares what you tell us you like with what we know about {CITIES.length} cities,
    across the continent's 54 countries. Here is how, and where our limits are.
  </p>

  <h2>A game, not a test</h2>
  <p>
    The questions compare your preferences with the description of the cities. They say nothing
    about who you are, and the site measures no personality trait. The percentage shows how closely
    a city matches your answers, nothing more.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each answer counts as a clue, and the clues multiply: an answer given at the end weighs as much
    as one given at the start, and still changes the list. The language you speak day to day weighs
    the most. A city far from one of your answers moves down, but it never disappears.
  </p>

  <h2>Sixteen criteria, a written grid</h2>
  <p>
    Each city gets a score from 0 to 5 on sixteen criteria. So that two people score a city the same
    way, each criterion has a grid: what 1, 3 and 5 mean (2 and 4 sit in between). Each score comes
    with a sentence that justifies it and with its sources, and their dates.
  </p>
  {gridsInFrench && (
    <p class="not">The grids below are still in French; their English version is on its way.</p>
  )}
  <dl class="criteria">
    {criteria.map((criterion) => (
      <div class="criterion" id={criterion.key}>
        <dt>{criterion.label}</dt>
        <dd>
          <p>{criterion.means}</p>
          <p class="not">Does not mean: {criterion.doesNotMean}</p>
          <ul class="rubric">
            <li>
              <b>1</b> {criterion.rubric["1"]}
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>3</b> {criterion.rubric["3"]}
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>5</b> {criterion.rubric["5"]}
            </li>
          </ul>
        </dd>
      </div>
    ))}
  </dl>

  <h2 id="sources">Where the figures come from</h2>
  <p>
    For each country, a research file gathers dated sources: French and UK travel advice, local
    press, property listings, Afrobarometer surveys, the official lists of French schools,
    operators' websites. For each city, notes add to this file, neighbourhood by neighbourhood.
  </p>
  <p>Each score says where it comes from:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <b>Measured</b>: a figure published for the city (rent listings, a number of schools).
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Survey</b>: what residents say in a survey, often for the whole region.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Sourced</b>: our reading of the grid, backed by named and dated sources.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Estimate</b>: no source specific to the city; it is our judgement.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Today, {percentOf(unknown)}% of the scores are unknown and {percentOf(estimates)}% are
    estimates. An unknown score counts as an average score (2.5): it neither helps nor hurts a city,
    and it is never given as a reason. When a city rests on too many unknowns for your answers, its
    card says so.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our sources do not always agree: a local voice, a specialist site and an official site can see
    the same city differently. We pass on what they say, as of their date. On the ground, things can
    vary from one neighbourhood to the next and from one month to the next. When a line says "no
    neighbourhood", it means none of the neighbourhoods in our notes fits: the city may well have
    others.
  </p>

  <h2>Travel advice</h2>
  <p>
    For each city, we give the advice of the French foreign ministry (France Diplomatie) and that of
    the UK (FCDO), with their dates. This advice removes no city and does not change its score: it
    is written for travellers who do not know the city, while people live, work and come back there.
    Today, the authorities advise against travel to {advisedAgainst} cities out of {CITIES.length}.
    For those, we suggest you ask people who live there which precautions to take. Everyday safety
    remains a scored criterion, with its sources, like the others.
  </p>

  <h2>What we never score</h2>
  <p>
    How residents welcome returnees from the diaspora, women or LGBT people is not scored: a score
    would rank people's dignity, and it would rest on anecdotes. We describe it, with a dated
    source, in the "Good to know" section or on the city's page. The same goes for religion and for
    food.
  </p>

  <h2 id="music">The music that gets you moving</h2>
  <p>
    In the full questionnaire, if you like going out, you can say which music makes you dance. It
    brings the countries where it was born or fills the dance floors a little closer, never more: it
    is one reason among others. Each link has its source.
  </p>
  <ul>
    {MUSIC.map((genre) => (
      <li>
        <b>{GENRES_EN[genre.label] ?? genre.label}</b>:{" "}
        {genre.countries.map((country) => countryName(country, "en")).join(", ")} (
        {genre.sources.map((source, i) => (
          <>
            {i > 0 && "; "}
            <a href={source.url} rel="noopener noreferrer">
              {source.title}
            </a>
          </>
        ))}
        )
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2>Our limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The big capitals lead on jobs, flights, internet and healthcare. To bring a smaller city
      forward, say so: a calm pace, a tight budget.
    </li>
    <li>
      Many figures hold for a region or for the whole country. The justification says so ("at
      country level").
    </li>
    <li>Rents are asking prices. They are often negotiable.</li>
    <li>
      The map comes from Natural Earth (public domain): the borders are those of today's states, and
      the way they are drawn takes no side in disputes.
    </li>
    <li>
      Travel advice changes quickly. Before you go, always check your government's page (in the UK,
      the FCDO).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A mistake, a more recent source? Write to us:
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.en}`}>{CONTACT.en}</a>.
  </p>
</Page>

<style>
  .criteria {
    display: grid;
    gap: 14px;
    margin: 16px 0 0;
  }
  .criterion {
    background: var(--night-deep);
    border-radius: 14px;
    padding: 14px 16px;
  }
  dt {
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }
  dd {
    margin: 6px 0 0;
  }
  dd p {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
  }
  .not {
    color: var(--thread-dim);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }
  .rubric {
    margin: 6px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    gap: 4px;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }
  .rubric b {
    display: inline-block;
    width: 1.4em;
    color: var(--saffron);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }
</style>
